{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pon-06-three-digit-number-puzzles",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Three-Digit Number Puzzles",
    "description": "Extend place-value reasoning to hundreds, tens and units, including swaps, full reversal and digit-relation constraints.",
    "teachingApproach": "Extend the familiar tens-and-units model to 100h+10t+u, then derive the change caused by a tens-units swap and by a full reversal. Use those derived patterns with multiplicative digit relations, digit sums, equal outer digits, percentage relations and a reversal capstone. Digit bounds are treated as mathematical constraints, not an afterthought.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "Teach a three-digit number as 100h+10t+u. Show from place value that swapping tens and units changes the number by 9(u−t), while reversing hundreds and units changes it by 99(u−h). Cover multiplicative and percentage relations among digits, digit-sum conditions, a middle digit equal to the sum of the outer digits, equal hundreds and units digits, and counting valid digit combinations. Keep h nonzero and all digits integral from 0 to 9. End with a capstone combining a digit sum and a full-reversal change."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's extend place value one more step.\n\nThree-digit puzzles look busier, but every clue still talks about only three positions: hundreds, tens and units. Model those positions correctly and the swaps become simple multiples of 9 or 99.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A three-digit number has three place values.\n\nHundreds digit h contributes 100h.\nTens digit t contributes 10t.\nUnits digit u contributes u.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "So the number is:\n100h + 10t + u\n\nFor 472:\n400 + 70 + 2 = 472\n\nThe hundreds digit h cannot be 0, because then the number would not be three-digit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
